<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thirteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Thirteenth Amendment</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thirteenth Amendment to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">United States Constitution</a> abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and involuntary servitude, except as punishment for crime. Passed by Congress in January 1865 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-lincoln">Abraham Lincoln</a>'s support and ratified in December 1865, it freed the remaining enslaved people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It was the first of three Reconstruction amendments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment.jpg" alt="Thirteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Archives of the United States · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:13th_Amendment_Pg1of1_AC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reconstruction readmitted the states after they ratified the <b>Thirteenth Amendment</b>, which outlawed slavery &quot;except as a punishment for crime&quot;; in Texas v. White (1869) the Supreme Court ruled that no state could unilaterally secede, and in Sprott v. United States (1874) that the Confederacy's foundation was treason.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the American Civil War Abraham Lincoln’s Emancipation Proclamation of 1 January 1863 declared free the enslaved in the rebel states, some 180,000 Black men served in the Union army, and the <b>Thirteenth Amendment</b>, ratified in December 1865, ended slavery throughout the country.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The abolition of slavery by the <b>Thirteenth Amendment</b> in 1865 was followed by the Fourteenth and Fifteenth Amendments, which promised citizenship, the equal protection of the laws and the vote regardless of race.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Georgia ratified the <b>Thirteenth Amendment to the United States Constitution</b>, which made slavery illegal except as punishment for a crime; that day it reached the required 27 of the then 36 states.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-6">6 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
